{# The status page lives on the public site on purpose: GitHub Pages is not the server it
   reports on, so this page still loads when that server does not, and says so. The checks
   themselves run on the API every five minutes (api src/status.js); this page only reads them.
   Design language: references/design-language.md in the portfolio skill. #}
<div class="an-wrap px-4 py-10 sm:px-6">
  <header class="mb-6">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-brand-text">Status</h1>
    <p class="mt-1 max-w-prose text-sm text-brand-muted">
      Every part of this site and the services it depends on, checked every five minutes. This
      page is served separately, from GitHub Pages, so it still loads when the rest is down.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div id="stBanner" class="card st-banner mb-6" role="status" aria-live="polite" data-state="loading">
    <span class="st-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    <span id="stBannerText" class="font-semibold text-brand-text">Checking…</span>
  </div>

  <section class="card mb-4">
    <div class="an-panel-head">
      <h2 class="an-panel-title">This site and app</h2>
      <p class="text-xs text-brand-muted">Last 24 hours, one cell an hour</p>
    </div>
    <ul id="stStack" class="mt-2 divide-y divide-brand-border"></ul>
  </section>

  <section class="card mb-4">
    <div class="an-panel-head">
      <h2 class="an-panel-title">Services the app depends on</h2>
      <p class="text-xs text-brand-muted">Last 24 hours, one cell an hour</p>
    </div>
    <ul id="stUpstream" class="mt-2 divide-y divide-brand-border"></ul>
  </section>

  <p class="text-xs text-brand-muted">
    Transit is recorded from the app's real requests rather than probed, so that checking it
    spends none of its quota; between trips it reads "no recent data". Checks run from the same
    server as the API, so if that server is down, only this page can tell you.
    <span id="stUpdated"></span>
  </p>
</div>
